        src/library.c -> src/library.next.c, by re-running rather than merging.

            sh kit/compact/rebuild

        Everything this does is a pass in here, so a library.c that other
        people have been editing is kept up with by running it again. There
        is nothing to reconcile by hand, and a pass whose anchor has gone
        says so and leaves the rest to run.

            fixes.py        two corrections, anchored on the file's own
                            wording. See src/test/native/FINDING.
            chain.py        instructions several to a source line
            dedup.py        a routine's contract written once, not once per
                            architecture
            restructure.py  the moonwater_ prefix off, the C that only
                            forwarded gone, libc names as aliases
            parity.py       the routines an architecture was missing
            inventory.py    the map at the top, read off the result

            prove           assembles two versions for x86_64, arm64 and
                            riscv64 and compares the instructions and the
                            section bytes
            strip.py        removes assembly comments that only restate the
                            code; it stays a reviewed tool, not a rebuild pass.

        Only chain and dedup claim to change nothing, and rebuild proves it
        by running the whole pipeline twice, with and without them, and
        comparing. The other four move code on purpose.

        prove needs a clang with all three targets. Apple's has two of them;
        homebrew llvm has all three, and is where prove looks by default.

        On an arm64 machine rebuild finishes by running src/test/native/run,
        which puts two and a third million calls through the arm64 routines
        against a C reference. x86_64 and riscv64 are assembled and compared
        here and executed nowhere: that still wants qemu.
